  "worked_solution": "Recall that every quadratic form defined on ${R}^{3}$ has an associated $3\\times 3$ symmetric matrix.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each matrix in turn.\n\n- $T$ could be a matrix of a quadratic form defined on ${R}^{3}$ since it is a $3\\times 3$ symmetric matrix.\n\n- $U$ could also be a matrix of a quadratic form defined on ${R}^{3}$ since it is a $3\\times 3$ symmetric matrix.\n\n- $V$ *cannot* be a matrix of a quadratic form defined on ${R}^{3}$ since it's a $2\\times 2$ matrix (not $3\\times 3$).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$T$ and $U$ only.\"",
